   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the town law, in relation to the Peconic Bay region community\npreservation fund; and providing for the repeal of certain provisions upon\nexpiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S6847",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6847-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §64-e, Town L",
    "lawSection": "Town Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S6847\n\nSPONSOR: PALUMBO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the town law, in relation to the Peconic Bay region\ncommunity preservation fund; and providing for the  repeal of certain\nprovisions upon expiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo strengthen the Peconic Bay Community Preservation Fund provisions\nunder section 64-e of the town law as it relates to historic preserva-\ntion and community housing.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Amends subdivision 5 of section 64-e of the town law to\ninclude demonstrated experience in historic preservation activities in\naddition to conservation and land preservation activities or water qual-\nity improvement activities as one of the qualifications for the advisory\ncommittee.\n\nSubdivision 9 of section 64-e of the town law is amended to provide that\nlands acquired pursuant to the Peconic Bay Community Preservation Fund\nfor historic preservation and cultural purposes must be administered in\na manner that permits the adaptive reuse of such properties that is\nconsistent with the historic character of the property, while maximizing\npublic use to the maximum extent practicable.\n\nSection 64-e of the town law subdivision 10 is also amended to allow\ntowns to utilize development rights acquired with monies from the Pecon-\nic Bay Community Preservation Fund to provide community housing,\nprovided that such development rights have not been expressly extin-\nguished at the time of acquisition. Such lands shall remain preserved in\nperpetuity by a permanent conservation easement or other instrument that\nsimilarly preserves the community character referenced in subdivision 4\nof section 64-e of the town law. Where such development rights are sold\nfor community housing, the proceeds from such sale shall be deposited in\nthe community preservation fund.  For purposes of this subdivision,\n\"community housing\" shall mean a primary residential property where the\npurchase price limit shall not exceed 150% of the purchase price limits\nestablished by the state of New York mortgage agency low interest rate\nloan program in non-target categories for the county.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe Peconic Bay Region Community Preservation Fund law (CPF) permits the\ncreation of dedication land preservation funds by each of the five East\nEnd Towns (Southampton, East Hampton, Riverhead, Shelter Island and\nSouthold) for the protection of farmland, open space and community char-\nacter.\n\nIt was established by voter referendum in 1998, when voters in the five\nEast End towns of Long Island approved a new real estate transfer tax of\n2% on each transaction occurring in these towns. This is a onetime tax\nthat the buyer of real estate is required to pay when purchasing\nimproved or vacant property.\n\nIn 2006, voters in all five townships approved a referendum to extend\nthe collection of the tax from 2020 to 2030 and then on November 8,\n2016, voters in the five East End towns extended the CPF to 2050 and\nalso added the opportunity for each town to invest up to 20% of the\nfunds toward water quality improvement projects. Most recently in 2020,\nlegislation was enacted to expand the definition of preservation of\ncommunity character under the Peconic Bay Community Preservation Fund to\ninclude projects that will help preserve and protect the maritime herit-\nage of Long Island's East End.\n\nSince the inception of the Peconic Bay Community Preservation Fund in\n1999, the 2% real estate transfer tax for land acquisition for open\nspace, farmland and historic preservation, as well as recreational\npurposes, has been successful in generating over $1.655 billion and has\nprotected over 10,000 acres of land on the East End of Long Island that\notherwise would have been lost to development.\n\nThis legislation clarifies the existing law by making it clear that\ndevelopment rights acquired under CPF, which have not been expressly\nextinguished at the time of acquisition, can be utilized to provide\ncommunity housing at another suitable location. The COVID-19 pandemic\nhas further exacerbated the need for increased affordable housing oppor-\ntunities on the East End. Communities have seen sudden increases in\npopulation due to influxes of people leaving more densely populated\nurban areas to more rural areas which has driven up housing costs and\nreduced the supply of housing units available for the local workforces.\nThis legislation seeks to address this concern by amending the provision\nof the town law that relates to the Peconic Bay CPF to allow for the\nutilization of development rights acquired with monies from the CPF to\nprovide community housing, provided that such development rights have\nnot been expressly extinguished at the time of acquisition.\n\nOther changes to the CPF pertain to historic preservation. This legis-\nlation adds historic preservation to the list of experiences that quali-\nfy for membership on each town's CPF advisory board. It also permits the\nadaptive reuse of historic properties that is consistent with the\nhistoric character of the property, while maximizing public use to the\nextent practicable\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
